**Exploring Logo Integration**

Okay, I'm thinking about how the user can integrate their image logo.  I've already created a `Logo.tsx` component as a foundation. Now, I'm trying to figure out the best way to explain the image upload process for both the logo and app icon.  My next step is crafting clear, concise instructions.


**Refining Image Integration**

I've been thinking about the limitations of direct file uploads. I'm focusing on providing the user with practical methods for integrating their logo, given I cannot host the file. I will explain image integration by updating the `src` attribute. I'll provide clear instructions about hosting the image online (S3, Cloudinary, public URL) and how to update the `Logo.tsx` file I generated.



**Outlining Integration Steps**

I'm now thinking about how to best communicate the integration process. Since I can't directly receive a file, I'll need to explain the steps. First, the user needs to host their image online, using something like Imgur or their own server. Then, they simply paste that URL into the `Logo.tsx` file.


**Devising Integration Solutions**

I'm now thinking about how to provide alternative upload options. I'm considering providing a temporary placeholder image, but realize this isn't the core of their request.  I've decided against base64 encoding due to its inefficiency for larger files. Instead, I'll focus on demonstrating direct editing of the `Logo.tsx` component with an easy URL input. I'll provide an explanation and then re-provide the code, making the comments more explicit for ease of pasting.


**Providing URL Integration**

I've refined the instructions for logo integration further.  Now, I'll provide an updated `Logo.tsx` file with clear placeholders for the image URL.  I've decided to keep text fallback in case the image fails to load.  The updated file has explicit instructions about public URLs and where to paste the links.

```typescript
import React from 'react';

interface LogoProps {
  className?: string;
  variant?: 'full' | 'icon';
  colored?: boolean;
}

const Logo: React.FC<LogoProps> = ({ className = "", variant = 'full', colored = true }) => {
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 🟢 INSTRUCTIONS:
  // 1. Upload your logo and icon to a public URL (e.g., Imgur, S3, Cloudinary).
  // 2. Replace the placeholders below with the URLs of your images.
  // 3. Save the file.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  const logoSrc = "PUT_YOUR_LOGO_URL_HERE";
  const iconSrc = "PUT_YOUR_ICON_URL_HERE";

  let logoText = colored ? 'my text' : 'my text'; // Default text if image fails

  if (variant === 'full') {
    return (
      <div className={`flex items-center gap-2 ${className}`}>
        <img
          src={logoSrc}
          alt="My Logo"
          onError={(e) => {
            (e.target as HTMLImageElement).src = ''; // Clear image if load fails
          }}
          className="h-8"
        />
        <span>{logoText}</span>
      </div>
    );
  }
  else {
    return (
      <img
          src={iconSrc}
          alt="My App Icon"
          onError={(e) => {
            (e.target as HTMLImageElement).src = ''; // Clear image if load fails
          }}
          className="h-8"
      />
    );
  }
};

export default Logo;
```


**Improving Integration Instructions**

I've been refining my approach to explain the logo integration process. I'm focusing on simplicity and clarity. I will guide the user to provide the correct image URL and specify how to add images if developing locally (e.g. Next.js). I'm also ensuring the code examples are easy to paste and understand.


**Updating Logo Integration**

I'm now writing an updated `Logo.tsx` component that explains how to use an image URL or a local image path. I have added comments to help guide the user through the process, specifying how to add images if developing locally (e.g. Next.js). I'm confident this will simplify their integration of a logo.


**Simplifying Integration Logic**

I'm currently thinking of simplifying the logic for handling logo integration. I'm going to provide the user with two image URL input fields for the logo and the icon. Then, I'll switch between the image and the text based on the presence of the logo and icon URLs provided by the user. I will be sure to add comments and documentation that explain how this works.

```typescript
// Paste URL here, e.g., "https://mysite.com/logo.png"
const logoUrl = ""; // Paste URL here, e.g., "https://mysite.com/logo.png"
const iconUrl = ""; // Paste URL here, e.g., "https://mysite.com/icon.png"

const hasCustomLogo = logoUrl.length > 0 && iconUrl.length > 0;

if (hasCustomLogo) {
  if (variant === 'icon') {
    return (
      <img
        src={iconUrl}
        alt="Tamareen Icon"
        className={`object-contain ${className}`}
        style={{ maxHeight: '100%', maxWidth: '100%' }}
      />
    );
  }
  return (
    <img
      src={logoUrl}
      alt="Tamareen Logo"
      className={`object-contain ${className}`}
      style={{ maxHeight: '100%', maxWidth: '100%' }}
    />
  );
}

// ==============================================================================
// 🟠 FALLBACK: CSS / TEXT LOGO
// ==============================================================================
```


**Finalizing Icon Integration**

I'm now integrating a three-bar icon mark and considering how to customize it. I'll provide an alternative `iconUrl` if no other URLs are provided. I'm building a text-based logo fallback for better customization. I'll add the default text in an `h1` tag.


**Refactoring Icon Handling**

I'm now refactoring the `Logo.tsx` component to make it easier to customize the icon and logo appearance. I'm introducing explicit URL variables for both the logo and icon, providing better control over their display. I'll provide an example to show how it can be customized.


**Optimizing Image Rendering**

I'm presently figuring out how to render the images effectively. I'm focusing on providing the URL fields. This reduces unnecessary complexity and gives the user full control. I will provide a method to customize the URL and text, so it's a seamless integration.

